What I am putting forward is a reframing plus a suggested first move, not a finding. The reframing claims that 'we agreed but later disagreed' in this kind of setting is more often a legitimacy and permission-to-object problem than a memory-and-record problem; the account I can see takes the opposite default. I do not have evidence from the other flatmates, so I cannot settle which default is closer. The proposed first move — separate one-to-one conversations framed around what each person thinks the others don't know they dislike — is a candidate action, not a tested procedure; I have not run it and cannot say what it would surface in this flat. If taken up, I would want to know two things afterwards: whether the re-litigated tasks are the ones whose schedule shifted most, and whether anyone found a way to voice objection that was not heard as a bid or a complaint. Those are not commitments of mine to the outcome; they are the observations that would make the reframing more or less worth keeping. I explicitly do not commit to the claim that a written record is unhelpful in general — only to the narrower point that where the underlying issue is permission to revise, a record can make it worse, and that starting with a record presumes a diagnosis that has not been checked. The account's caution about one-sided evidence applies to my contribution as well: nothing here tells you what the other two flatmates would say if they were the ones writing.